Patchsu patch 502 (optional): McOsu mods ported to lazer (osu! ruleset): Mafham and Click Control.

Needs 0001 only. Also applies together with every other patch (in glob order).

Mod select shows a "from McOsu" plaque on both mods (the "M" circle of patch 500's "from Modosu" plaque, green text).
Both mods have their own mod icons (embedded in osu.Game: Textures/Icons/Mods/mcosu-*.png).

Mafham (MH, Fun): "Maybe infinity IS finite after all?" McOsu's approach rate of negative infinity.
  Every object except spinners is on screen at once, without approach circles, follow points or the "not hittable yet" dim
  (McOsu turns that off too: it would show what comes next). Gameplay doesn't change: hit windows, stacking and autoplay stay
  as they are (only the reading part of star rating and PP is dropped, see below). Like McOsu, the whole map appears together 3 s (real time) before the first object, after
  another 3 s of lead-in in which it's drawn invisibly. When an object becomes playable, its picture is swapped for its
  drawable in the same frame; both look the same. Circles, slider ends and repeats don't flash in kiai sections (pictures can't).
  A retry of the same map (same mods, skin and window) reuses the first play's pictures: the map appears 1 s after the retry
  instead of 3 s. Song select and the mod select footer show AR as −∞ (green, a reduction); this is display only.
  Below 100% far objects brightness, the brightness is written as a small exponent after it (−∞^.5 at 50%); it rolls
  from one value to the next like the value itself.
  Setting "Far objects brightness" (100% – 10%) darkens the objects that aren't within the normal approach rate yet, so the next
  ones stand out (each brightens over 200 ms when it gets there). While watching a replay (or autoplay), clicking its icon
  switches it off: the far objects fade to 10%, and each fades in over 200 ms when it becomes playable. Own toggle, no 0005
  needed (0005's replay toggles don't include it). Reading doesn't count with Mafham: no reading in its star rating or PP.
  Incompatible with mods that animate, move or rotate objects or need approach circles (Hidden, Traceable, Approach Different,
  Freeze Frame, Transform, Wiggle, Spin In, Grow/Deflate, Magnetised, Repel, Depth, Barrel Roll).

Click Control (CC, Difficulty Increase). McOsu's "Jigsaw 1": a click that doesn't judge anything counts as a miss.
  Like McOsu: it breaks combo, lowers accuracy and health, counts as a miss (also for Sudden Death / Perfect), and there's
  no limit, so a play can have more misses than objects. The map's max combo stays the same, the miss isn't on hit error
  meters, and a red miss shows at the cursor (plus the combo break sound). Clicks don't count before the first object has
  ended, after the last one, in breaks, in gaps over 5 s, as a spinner's first click, or when resuming from pause. Clicking
  a locked circle (notelock shake) or outside every hit window counts. Replays recreate the misses; rewinding takes them back.
  Incompatible with Relax, Alternate and Single Tap.

How it works:
- Click Control: osu.Game.Rulesets.Osu/Mods/OsuModClickControl.cs. A component first in line for key presses notes each
  press; one updated right after input checks whether the press produced any judgement and, if not, applies a Miss for a
  ClickControlMiss object (empty hit windows) to the score and health processors. Its judgement is an ExtraJudgement
  (osu.Game/Rulesets/Judgements/ExtraJudgement.cs): JudgementProcessor doesn't count those towards JudgedHits (so the map
  still completes), and ScoreProcessor doesn't make it the "previous object" of the next hit event.
- Mafham: osu.Game.Rulesets.Osu/Mods/OsuModMafham.cs. The preempt is lengthened on the gameplay copy of the beatmap only,
  after the lead-in and autoplay were worked out; the playfield's drawables keep their normal lifetimes. Everything further
  ahead is drawn by MafhamLayer: pages of objects drawn once into cached frame buffers from frozen copies (drawable hit
  objects on a stopped clock). The front page holds 16 objects, later ones double up to 64 and are split when they come to
  the front, so taking an object out redraws a few objects at most. Only the first two pages keep their copies; the others
  keep just the picture (a slider body alone holds ~1 MB of renderer buffers) and get copies back in the background before
  they're needed. Copies are drawn into a page with correct coverage (alpha "over", not o!f's summed alpha), so a page holds
  premultiplied colours; it's drawn as such, with its draw colour premultiplied too (a fading page, e.g. the player fading out
  on exit or restart, fades like live objects instead of being added on top, white). Pages aren't redrawn while scaled
  (player open/close and fail animations stretch them), and are redrawn once the window size settles. A page is placed on
  whole screen pixels when it's drawn: a frame buffer is its drawable's screen size rounded up, drawn back with nearest
  filtering, so on fractional pixels the picture shifted by up to a pixel and lost a row/column (looked compressed). The layer stays
  invisible until every page is drawn, then fades in at once. A picture is taken out in the frame its drawable comes alive.
  A 3-minute map with ~960 objects plays with the same draw calls / frame times as without the mod (every object live
  instead: ~6400 draw calls, 14 fps).
- Kept pictures: once every page is drawn, each page's picture is copied into a Snapshot (a cached frame buffer drawn once from
  a BufferedContainerView of the page, then taken out of the draw tree; its frame buffer lives until it's disposed). The last
  map's snapshots stay in a static PictureCache (keyed by beatmap, object positions, mods, skin and beatmap skin/colour settings,
  checked against each page's screen rectangle). A retry's pages draw a view of their snapshot (one quad each); only the first
  two pages load copies, for when objects are taken out. Old snapshots are disposed once another map's are kept and no play
  uses them. Costs about as much GPU memory as the pages themselves while kept.
- IApplicableToGameplayStartTime (osu.Game/Rulesets/Mods): lets a mod lengthen the lead-in (DrawableOsuRuleset uses it).
- −∞ display: OsuRuleset.GetAdjustedDisplayDifficulty (display only, never used for calculations) returns negative infinity for
  AR with Mafham; the approach time reads "∞ ms". VerticalAttributeDisplay doesn't roll to or from an infinite value (it would
  show NaN), and the mod select footer, song select / results statistics and the two tooltips write "−∞" (.NET writes "-Infinity").
  The brightness exponent is RulesetBeatmapAttribute.ValueExponent (set on AR by OsuRuleset.GetBeatmapAttributesForDisplay),
  drawn by VerticalAttributeDisplay and BeatmapTitleWedge.StatisticDifficulty with AttributeExponentCounter (osu.Game/Overlays/Mods:
  a RollingCounter, 250 ms like the value; invariant culture, no leading zero; hidden at 1 / none).
- Replay toggle: OsuModMafham.ReplayToggle (added to the HUD in replays) looks for Mafham's ModIcons twice a second and keeps
  invisible click areas over them; a click switches the pictures' opacity and refreshes the live drawables' state.
- Reading: OsuDifficultyCalculator sets the reading difficulty to 0 with Mafham (after its note count, so PP's miss penalty is
  unchanged); the star rating and PP's reading value both come from it.
- Slider-heavy maps: page sizes are counted in cost (a slider costs 3 circles), copies get their costly first update a few per
  frame (3 ms budget) before their page is drawn, and copies are disposed on o!f's background disposal thread (disposing a
  page of slider copies on the update thread took up to 200 ms).
- DrawableOsuHitObject.DimUntilHittable (Mafham turns it off).
- Plaque: mods marked IMcOsuMod (osu.Game/Rulesets/Mods) get McOsuModPlaque (osu.Game/Overlays/Mods), added by ModPanel's
  constructor (0500/0501 add theirs in load / LoadComplete). Its own copy of the icon, so it doesn't need 0500.
- Leak fix: a non-pooled drawable hit object's SyntheticHitObjectEntry stops following its hit object when the drawable is
  disposed (HitObjectLifetimeEntry.StopFollowingHitObject); before, the hit object kept every such drawable alive.

